@using BulkyBook.Models



@* (32) This is the model that we get from the CategoryController  *@
@model List<Category>

@* <h1>Category List</h1>
 *@

@* (31) We will display here the categories that we receive from the database
This is a Razor page wherein we can combine html and c# code.
The C# code is to be separated by @ sign.

But how will the controller know that we have available list of categories?
We passed the categories in the Index method in the CategoryController.
This is done using model as shown in (32)
*@


@* @foreach (var category in Model)
{
    <p>@category.Name</p> 
    // here if you notice, we have to write the @ again. This is because we are in HTML.
    // Whenever we are in HTML, to add C# code, we use @ sign.

 *@


@* (33) We will now style the application.
    For that we use the resources present in Course Content Folder.
    1. Create a images folder in wwwroot which will contain the images. This all will be static content 
*@

@* ********************************************************************************************************************* *@


@* (42) Styling the category index page, everything before this is commented at this step *@

<div class="container-fluid py-4">

    @* (86) Displaying success message if TempData is not null. Test this and update a category, you will be able to see a message. 
        TempData is loaded just for one request, when you refresh the page the message will be gone.

        Going ahead there will be messages for failure. Also we might need to show messages on all the pages.
        It means we might need to copy paste this at all the pages where we want to display this.
        This does not follow DRY- Do not repeat yourself principle. For this we can make use of the partial views.
        Will comment this and add it in the _Notification.cshtml in (87)
    *@
   @*  @if(TempData["success"]!=null)
    {
        <div class="alert alert-success alert-dismissible fade show" role="alert">@TempData["success"]</div>
    } *@

    @* (90)  With this single line, wherever we have to display TempData or notificiation, we can add partial
        views like this. This is beneficial as if we have to change something in the future, we only have to update one file 
        which is _Notification.cshtml
    *@
    @* <partial name="_Notification" /> *@

@*     (99) To make the notification accessbile from all pages and not just index, we will comment it from here and add it in the _Layout.cshtml file.
    This way we can access it from all pages.  <partial name="_Notification" /> this above is commented at this step 99 and added at 100*@
    <div class="card border shadow-sm overflow-hidden">
        <div class="card-header text-white py-3 px-4 d-flex align-items-center justify-content-between" style="background: linear-gradient(to right, #065f46, #282828, #191414);">
            <div>
                <h5 class="mb-0 fw-bold"><i class="bi bi-grid me-2"></i>Categories</h5>
                <p class="mb-0 small" style="color: #1DB954;">Manage your product categories</p>
            </div>
            @* (43) Adding the categories *@
            <a asp-controller="Category" asp-action="Create" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light fw-semibold d-inline-flex align-items-center gap-1">
                <i class="bi bi-plus-lg"></i> New Category
            </a>
        </div>
        <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table-styled">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Category Name</th>
                        <th>Display Order</th>
                        <th class="text-end">Actions</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <!-- Sample Row - Students will replace with @@foreach loop -->
                    @* (43) Here we now add a C# for loop to display all the categories *@

                    @foreach (var category in Model)
                    {
                        <tr>
                            <td class="fw-medium" style="color: #1e293b;">@category.Name</td>
                            <td><span class="badge bg-light text-dark border">@category.DisplayOrder</span></td>
                            <td class="text-end">
                                @* (68) Adding update and edit functionality *@
                                @*(71) We will add the asp-route-nameGivenInControllerActionMethod to pass the id of the category to the Update and Delete methods in the CategoryController. 
                                This is required because we need to know which category to update or delete. *@
                                <a asp-action="Update" asp-route-id="@category.Id" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-success me-1"><i class="bi bi-pencil-square me-1"></i>Edit</a>
                                <a asp-action="Delete" asp-route-id="@category.Id" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-danger"><i class="bi bi-trash me-1"></i>Delete</a>
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                    }

                    
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

